\subsection{4.1, Determinants}

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        True or false: If <m>A</m> is a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix satisfying
        <md><mrow>Ae_1 = e_2, \qquad Ae_2 = e_2 + e_3, \qquad Ae_3 = e_2-e_3</mrow></md>,
        then
        <m>\det(A) = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        True.  We see
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}Ae_1 \amp Ae_2 \amp Ae_3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}
              \xrightarrow[]{R_2 = R_2 + R_3} \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        By
            doing just one row replacement (which does not change the
            determinant), we have obtained a triangular matrix with
            determinant <m>(0)(2)(-1)=0</m>, so <m>\det(A) = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>True or false: If <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix and the equation <m>Ax = b</m> has
      more than one solution for some <m>b</m> in <m>{\bf R}^n</m>, then <m>\det(A) = 0</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True.  Since <m>Ax=b</m> has infinitely many solutions for some <m>b</m>, we know <m>Ax=0</m>
        has infinitely many solutions.  Therefore, <m>A</m> is not invertible and so
        <m>\det(A)=0</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>True or false: If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <m>n \times n</m> matrices and <m>\det(AB) \neq 0</m>,
      then <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> must both be invertible.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: <m>\det(AB) = \det(A)\det(B)</m> and the quantity is nonzero by assumption,
        so both <m>\det(A)</m> and <m>\det(B)</m> must be nonzero, hence <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are both
        invertible.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>True or false: If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are <m>n \times n</m> matrices and <m>\det(A)=0</m>, then
      <m>AB</m> is not invertible.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: <m>\det(AB) = \det(A) \det(B) = 0 \det(B) = 0</m>, so <m>AB</m> cannot be
        invertible.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use row operations to find the determinant of the following matrix:
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 12 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We row-reduce <m>A</m> until we get a triangular matrix.
        <md>
          <mrow>\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 2 \amp 12 \amp 4</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}
          \amp  \xrightarrow[]{R_2= R_2/2} \begin{pmatrix}4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 6 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}
          \xrightarrow[R_4 = R_4-2R_2]{R_3 = R_3-R_2}
            \begin{pmatrix}4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 6 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp -7 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp -12 \amp -3\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  \xrightarrow[]{R_4 = R_4 - (12/7)R_3}
            \begin{pmatrix}4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 6 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp -7 \amp -1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -9/7\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
        </md>
        Let's call the final matrix <m>B</m>, which is upper-triangular (so <m>\det(B)</m> is the
        product of its diagonal entries).  To obtain <m>B</m> from <m>A</m>, we did a row scale of
        <m>1/2</m>, followed by a bunch of row replacements that don't affect determinants.
        Therefore,
        <md><mrow>\det(A)(1/2) = \det(B), \qquad \det(A)= 2 \det(B) = 2 \cdot 4(1)(-7)(-9/7) = 72</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the value of <m>h</m> that makes the determinant of the following matrix equal to <m>0</m>.
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \amp h\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The determinant is <m>0</m> if and only if the matrix is not invertible, so we row-reduce:
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \amp h\end{pmatrix}
          \xrightarrow[R_3 = R_3 - 2R_1]{R_2 = R_2 - R_1}
            \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp -2 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp -2 \amp h-6\end{pmatrix}
          \xrightarrow[]{R_3 = R_3 - R_2}
            \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp -2 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp h-4\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        This matrix is invertible unless it has fewer than <m>3</m> pivots.  Therefore, the
        determinant of the matrix is zero if and only if <m>h-4=0</m>, so <m>h=4</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If the matrix on the left has determinant <m>5</m>, what is the determinant of the
        matrix on the right?
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}a \amp b \amp c \\ d \amp e \amp f \\ g \amp h \amp i\end{pmatrix} \qquad
        \begin{pmatrix}g \amp h \amp i \\ d \amp e \amp f \\ a-d \amp b-e \amp c-f\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
      <p>The first operation was a row swap which switched the determinant from <m>5</m> to <m>-5</m>.
      The second operation was a row-replacement which did not affect the determinant.
      Therefore, the determinant of the final matrix is <m>-5</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We track the row operations required to obtain the final matrix from the original:
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}a \amp b \amp c \\ d \amp e \amp f \\ g \amp h \amp i\end{pmatrix}
          \xrightarrow[\textup{new det is }-5]{R_1 \leftrightarrow R_3} \begin{pmatrix}g \amp h \amp i \\ d \amp e \amp f \\ a \amp b \amp c\end{pmatrix}
          \xrightarrow[\textup{det is still } -5]{R_3=R_3-R_2} \begin{pmatrix}g \amp h \amp i \\ d \amp e \amp f \\ a-d \amp b-e \amp c-f\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>T</m> is a linear transformation with the property that <m>T \circ T = T</m>.
      What are the possibilities for the determinant of the standard matrix for <m>T</m>?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be the standard matrix for <m>T</m>, so <m>T(x) = Ax</m>.  Note
        <md><mrow>T(x) = (T \circ T)(x) = T(T(x)) = T(Ax)=A(Ax) = A^2 x</mrow></md>,
        so <m>A = A^2</m>.  Using
        this and the multiplicative property of determinants, we get
        <md><mrow>\det(A) = \det(A^2) = \det(A) \det(A) = (\det \ A)^2</mrow></md>.
        For <m>x = \det(A)</m>, the above says <m>x = x^2</m>, so <m>0 = x^2-x = x(x-1)</m>.  Therefore
        <m>x=0</m> or <m>x=1</m>, so <m>\det(A)</m> is <m>0</m> or <m>1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>Both <m>0</m> and <m>1</m> are possible for the determinant of <m>A</m>.  For example, the
      transformations <m>T(x) = \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}x</m> and <m>T(x) = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}x</m> both
      satisfy <m>T \circ T = T</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>T</m> is a linear transformation with the property that <m>T \circ T</m> is the
      identity transformation.  What is the determinant of the standard matrix for
      <m>T</m>?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> be the standard matrix for <m>T</m>.  The same argument as the previous
          exercise shows that the standard matrix for <m>T \circ T</m> is <m>A^2</m>.  But we were
          told <m>T \circ T</m> is the identity transformation, so we also know that its
          standard matrix is <m>I</m>, therefore <m>I = A^2</m>.  Now:
        <md><mrow>\det(I) = \det(A^2) \ \implies \  1 = \det(A)\det(A) \ \implies \  1 = (\det \ A)^2
            \ \implies \ \det(A) = \pm 1</mrow></md>.
        Both values for <m>\det(A)</m> are possible in practice.  For example, the matrices
          <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</m> have determinants <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>
          respectively, and both satisfy <m>A^2 = I</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>A</m> be the <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix satisfying <m>Ae_1= 3e_2</m>, <m>Ae_2= e_3</m>, and
      <m>Ae_3=e_1</m>.  Find <m>\det(A)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p><m>A=\begin{pmatrix}0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</m>.  You can compute <m>\det(A)=1</m> or just do two row
        swaps to get a diagonal matrix, so that <m>\det(A)=(-1)^2(3)=3</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is an invertible <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix and <m>\det(A) = 2</m>.
      Find <m>\det( (3A)^{-1} )</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        By properties of the determinant,
          <m>\det( (3A)^{-1} ) = \dfrac{1}{\det(3A)}</m>.  Since <m>3A</m> is obtained
          from <m>A</m> by multiplying \textit{each row} of <m>A</m> by <m>3</m>, we need to
          multiply the determinant by <m>3</m> twice.
        <md><mrow>\det(3A) = 3^2 \det(A) = 9 (2) = 18</mrow></md>.
        Therefore,
        <md><mrow>\det((3A)^{-1}) = \dfrac{1}{18}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>A problem from section 2.2, revisited:
      Find all
      values of <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> so that
      <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ c\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}d \\ 2\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m> is not
      <m>{\bf R}^2</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Two vectors in <m>{\bf R}^2</m> will fail to span <m>{\bf R}^2</m> precisely when
          they are linearly dependent, so we solve:
        <md><mrow>\det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp d \\ c \amp 2\end{pmatrix} = 0, \qquad 2 - cd = 0, \qquad cd = 2</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, <m>d = 2/c</m> where <m>c</m> is any nonzero number (the fact that
          <m>cd=2</m> means neither <m>c</m> or <m>d</m> can be zero).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Is there a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix (whose entries are real numbers) with the property
      that <m>A^2 = -I</m>?  If so, give an example.  If not, justify why there is no such
      matrix.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Yes.  Take <m>A</m> to be the matrix for rotation by <m>90^\circ</m> counterclockwise.
          Then <m>A^2</m> is rotation by <m>2 \cdot 90 ^\circ = 180^\circ</m>, which is the same
          as scalar multiplication by <m>-1</m>.  In other words, <m>A^2 = -I</m>.  We can verify
          this algebraically:
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Is there a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix (whose entries are real numbers) with the
      property that <m>A^2 = -I</m>?  If so, give an example.  If not, justify why there is
      no such matrix.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        No.  If <m>A^2 = -I</m> then
        <md><mrow>\det(A^2) = \det(-I) = \det\begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix} = -1</mrow></md>.
        But this would mean
        <md><mrow>\det(A^2) = -1, \quad (\det A)^2 = -1, \qquad \det(A) = \pm i</mrow></md>.
        This is impossible, since the determinant of every real matrix is real.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is natural to ask: why doesn't the above argument also
          show that we can never have <m>A^2 = -I</m> in the <m>2 \times 2</m> case?  Note that
          the <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>-I</m> has determinant equal to <m>1</m> (not <m>-1</m>) since
        <md><mrow>\det\begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{pmatrix} = (-1)^2 = 1</mrow></md>,
        so we do not obtain an impossible
          equality for <m>\det(A)</m> in the <m>2 \times 2</m> case, and in fact we can found an
          actual example of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> so that <m>A^2 = -I</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>A</m> is a square matrix and that <m>A^k</m> is invertible for some <m>k</m>.  Must
      it be true that <m>A</m> is invertible?  Justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Yes, <m>A</m> must be invertible.  We use the fact that a square matrix is
        invertible if and only if its determinant is nonzero.  Since <m>A^k</m> is
        invertible for some <m>k</m>, this means <m>\det(A^k) \neq 0</m>.  From the properties
        of determinants we know <m>\det(A^k) = (\det \ A)^k</m>, so the previous sentence
        means <m>(\det A)^k \neq 0</m>, thus <m>\det(A) \neq 0</m>.  Therefore, <m>A</m> is
        invertible.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>\setcounter{problem}{0}

\newpage

\subsection{4.2, Cofactor expansions}

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>True or false: The cofactor expansion of an <m>n \times n</m> matrix along the
        first row gives the negative of the cofactor expansion along the second row.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  The cofactor expansion gives the same determinant, no matter what
          row (or column) you use.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Use a cofactor expansion to find <m>\det(A)</m> for
      <m>A =  \begin{pmatrix}5 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 5 \amp -5 \amp 2 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We expand along the third row, which has only two nonzero entries.
        <md>
          <mrow>\det(A) \amp  = 0 C_{31} + 0 C_{32} + 1 C_{33} + 2 C_{34}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = 1(-1)^{3+3} \det\begin{pmatrix}5 \amp 1 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 2 \amp 4</mrow>
          <mrow>5 \amp -5 \amp 3\end{pmatrix} +
                    2(-1)^{3+4} \det \begin{pmatrix}5 \amp 1 \amp -3</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 2 \amp 3</mrow>
          <mrow>5 \amp -5 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = 5(6+20)-1(0-20)+1(0-10) \quad + \quad
                    (-2)\left(5(4+15)-1(0-15) +(-3)(0-10)\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = 140 - 2(140) = -140</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>As a general rule, which method for computing <m>n \times n</m> determinants is
      faster: row-reduction, or the cofactor expansion?  Justify your answer.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Row-reduction is generally much faster, particularly if the matrix is large.
        Cofactor expansions scale very poorly as <m>n</m> increases.  For example, in order
        to compute the determinant of a <m>5 \times 5</m> matrix using cofactors, we need
        to compute <m>5</m> different <m>4 \times 4</m> determinants.  Each of these
        <m>4 \times 4</m> determinants requires us to compute <m>4</m> different <m>3 \times 3</m>
        determinants, and each of the <m>3 \times 3</m> determinants requires us to compute
        <m>3</m> different <m>2 \times 2</m> determinants.  In essence, the <m>5 \times 5</m>
        cofactor expansion would involve <m>5! = 120</m> computations of <m>2 \times 2</m>
        determinants.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the determinant of the following matrix using one of the cofactor expansion formulas.
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\ -5 \amp 0 \amp 9\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We expand along the second column, since it has only one nonzero entry.
        <md><mrow>\det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\ -5 \amp 0 \amp 9\end{pmatrix} = 0C_{21} + 1 C_{22} + 0C_{32}
          = 1 (-1)^{2+2} \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -2 \\ -5 \amp 9\end{pmatrix} = 1(9-10) = -1</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find all real values of <m>h</m> (if there are any) so that the following set of
        vectors is a basis for <m>{\bf R}^{3}</m>.
        <md><mrow>\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -7 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}h \\ 4 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ h \\ 2\end{pmatrix}\right\}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The three vectors form a basis for <m>{\bf R}^{3}</m> if and only if they are
          linearly independent, so put them as columns of a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix <m>A</m>.
          Since <m>A</m> is square, the vectors are linearly independent if and only
          if the matrix is invertible, which is when <m>\det(A) \neq 0</m>.  Now:
        <md><mrow>\det(A) = 1(8+h) - h(-14-h) + 3(7-4) = 8+h+14h+h^2 + 9
            = h^2+15h+17</mrow></md>.
        This determinant is zero when
        <md><mrow>h = \dfrac{-15 \pm \sqrt{15^2-4(17)}}{2} = \dfrac{-15 \pm \sqrt{157}}{2}</mrow></md>.
        As established above, the vectors are linearly independent when the determinant
          is nonzero, which is all real numbers <m>h</m> {\bf except}
          <m>\dfrac{-15 + \sqrt{157}}{2}</m> and <m>\dfrac{-15 - \sqrt{157}}{2}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the determinant of the following matrix using one of the cofactor expansion formulas.
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ -7 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\ -5 \amp -1 \amp 9\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We use the first row.  The determinant of the matrix is
        <md><mrow>4\Big[1(9)-(-1)(-2)\Big] - 0 + (-2)\Big[(-7)(-1)-(-5)(1)\Big]
          = 4(9-2) - 2(7+5) = 28-24 = 4</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find <m>\det(A^3)</m> if <m>A= \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -3 \amp 4 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 20\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Using the cofactor expansion along the second row, we find
        <md><mrow>\det(A)=-2(-1)^5\det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -3 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 0 \amp 20\end{pmatrix}
              = 2\Big(20+3(-6)+2(-2)\Big)=2(20-18-4)=-4</mrow></md>,
        so
            <m>\det(A^3)=(-4)^3= -64</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the cofactor expansion of the determinant to find <m>\det(A)</m> for
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\ 4 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 5 \\ -1 \amp -3 \amp -4 \amp 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We use the expansion along the fourth column.
        <md>
          <mrow>\det(A)
          \amp  = 0C_{14} + 0C_{24} + 5 C_{34} + 0 C_{44}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = 5 (-1)^{3+4} \det\begin{pmatrix}-1 \amp 3 \amp 4</mrow>
          <mrow>4 \amp 1 \amp 3</mrow>
          <mrow>-1 \amp -3 \amp -4\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = -5\Big[-1(-4+9)-3(-16+3)+4(-12+1)\Big]</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = -5(-5+39-44) = -5(-10)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = 50</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use either the cofactor expansion or row-reduction to find <m>\det(A)</m> for
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}7 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 10 \amp 8 \amp -4 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 7\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The matrix <m>A</m> is almost upper-triangular already, so it may take very little
        row reduction to obtain an upper-triangular matrix.  This gives us the hint
        that row-reduction may be the faster way.</p>
      <p>
        The most natural cofactor expansion would be along the first column or last
          row, since each of those has only one nonzero term.
        <md>
          <mrow>\det(A) \amp  = 7 \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 10 \amp 8 \amp -4</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 7\end{pmatrix}
                      = 7 \cdot 7(-1)^{4+4} \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 3 \amp 4</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 10 \amp 8</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp   = 49 \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 3 \amp 4</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 10 \amp 8</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}
                     = 49(3)(-1)^{3+3} \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 3</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 10\end{pmatrix}
                      = 147(10-6) = 588</mrow>
        </md>.
        Row-reduction only requires one step.
        <md><mrow>A =
          \begin{pmatrix}7 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 2 \amp 10 \amp 8 \amp -4 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 7\end{pmatrix}
          \xrightarrow[]{R_3 = R_3 - 2R_2}
          \begin{pmatrix}7 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp -6 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 7\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        With just one row-replacement (which does not affect the determinant),
          we obtained an upper-triangular matrix, so
        <md><mrow>\det(A) = 7(1)(4)(3)(7) = 588</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>A problem from section 2.2, revisited: Find all values of <m>b</m> (if
      there are any) so that <m>\begin{pmatrix}b \\ -3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> is a linear combination of
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Since <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</m> are linearly independent, we
          know from the Increasing Span Criterion that
          <m>\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}b \\ -3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m> will span
          <m>{\bf R}^3</m> (in other words, the three vectors will be linearly
          independent) unless <m>\begin{pmatrix}b \\ -3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> is in the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>
          and <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</m>.  We form the matrix whose three columns are the
          vectors and set its determinant equal to <m>0</m>.
        <md><mrow>\det\begin{pmatrix}3 \amp 1 \amp b \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\ 2 \amp 4 \amp 2\end{pmatrix} = 0</mrow></md>,
        <md><mrow>3\big[0(2) - (-3)(4)\big] \ - \ 1\big[1(2)-(-3)(2)\big] \ + \
            b\big[1(4)-2(0)\big] = 0</mrow></md>,
        <md><mrow>3(12) - 1(8) + b(4) = 0, \qquad 28+4b = 0, \qquad b = -7</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For what values of <m>c</m> does the following matrix fail to be invertible?
        <md><mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}c \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp c \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp c\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We need <m>\det(A) = 0</m>.
        <md><mrow>0 = \det(A) = c(c^2-1) - 1(c-1) +1(1-c) = c^3-3c+2 = (c-1)^2(c+2)</mrow></md>.
        Thus
        <m>c=1</m> and <m>c = -2</m> make <m>A</m> fail to be invertible.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note: to factor <m>c^3-3c+2</m> above, we did long division after observing that
        <m>c=1</m> was a zero of <m>c^3-3c+2</m>.  However, we could have instead grouped terms
        using the common factor of <m>c-1</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>c(c^2-1)-1(c-1)+1(1-c)
          \amp  = c(c-1)(c+1)-1(c-1)+1(1-c)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = (c-1)\Big[c(c+1)-1-1\Big]</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = (c-1)\Big[c^2+c-2\Big]</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = (c-1)(c-1)(c+2)</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises> \setcounter{problem}{0}

\newpage

\subsection{4.3, Determinants and volumes}

<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose we are given two vectors <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> in <m>{\bf R}^{2}</m> and asked to compute
      the area of their associated parallelogram, but the computation yields <m>0</m> as the answer.
      What does this mean?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>If <m>0 = |\det\begin{pmatrix}u \amp v\end{pmatrix}|</m>, then <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are linearly dependent, so they do
        not actually form a parallelogram.  Instead, since they are on the same line
        through the origin, they only form a portion of a line, which has <m>0</m> area in
        <m>{\bf R}^{2}</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the area of the parallelogram determined by the vectors
        <md><mrow>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}, \quad v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The area is <m>\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \amp v_2\end{pmatrix} \right|=
        \left|\det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \\ -3 \amp 4\end{pmatrix}\right| = \left|1(4)-2(-3)\right| = 10</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the area of the parallelogram with vertices
        <md><mrow>(1, 2), \quad (3, 7), \quad (4, -1),  \quad (6, 4)</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>To use the formula from section 4.2, we need to find vectors <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m>
        that determine the area of the parallelogram. For this, we can start at any
        vertex (we will use the first vertex) and find the vectors to each of its two
        adjacent vertices within the parallelogram.</p>
      <p>The vector from <m>(1,2)</m> to <m>(3, 7)</m> is <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 5\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      <p>The vector from <m>(1,2)</m> to <m>(4, -1)</m> is <m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ -3\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      <p>
        The area is
        <md><mrow>\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \amp v_2\end{pmatrix}\right| = \left|\det\begin{pmatrix}2 \amp 3 \\ 5 \amp -3\end{pmatrix}\right| =
            |2(-3)-3(5)| = |-21| = 21</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the area of the triangle with vertices
        <md><mrow>(4,-5), \quad (1, 2), \quad (-10, -3)</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The area is half of the area of the corresponding parallelogram.  To use the
        formula from section 4.2, we need to find vectors <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m> that
        determine the triangle. For this, we can start at any vertex
        (we will use the first vertex) and find the vectors to each of its two
        adjacent vertices within the triangle.</p>
      <p>The vector from <m>(4, -5)</m> to <m>(1, 2)</m> is <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}-3 \\ 7\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      <p>The vector from <m>(4, -5)</m> to <m>(-10, -3)</m> is <m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}-14 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      <p>
        The area is
        <md>
          <mrow>\dfrac{1}{2} \left|\det\begin{pmatrix}v_1 \amp v_2\end{pmatrix}\right|
            \amp  = \dfrac{1}{2} \left|\det\begin{pmatrix}-3 \amp -14</mrow>
          <mrow>7 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}\right|
            = \dfrac{1}{2}\left|(-3)(2)-(-14)(7)\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \dfrac{1}{2}\left|-6 + 98\right| = \dfrac{1}{2}(92) = 46</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the volume of the parallelepiped <m>P</m> naturally determined by the three
        vectors below.
        <md><mrow>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \quad v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 7 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}, \quad v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 4 \\ 6\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The volume is
        <md><mrow>\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 7 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 6\end{pmatrix}\right| =
          |1(7(6)-3(4)) - (-1)(0-0) + 2(0-0)| = 30</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the volume of the parallelepiped <m>P</m> naturally determined by the four
        vectors below.
        <md><mrow>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 4 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}, \quad v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 20 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \quad v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ -1 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix},
          \quad v_4 = \begin{pmatrix}5 \\ 5 \\ 3 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The volume is
        <md>
          <mrow>\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 5</mrow>
          <mrow>4 \amp 20 \amp -1 \amp 5</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 3</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 4\end{pmatrix}\right|
          \amp  = \left| 0C_{21} + 20C_{22} + 1C_{32} + 0 C_{42}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \left| 20 \det\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp -1 \amp 5</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 1 \amp 3</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 1 \amp 4\end{pmatrix} +
            1(-1)^{3+2} \det \begin{pmatrix}4 \amp -1 \amp 5</mrow>
          <mrow>4 \amp -1 \amp 5</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 0 \amp 4\end{pmatrix}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \left| 20 \Big[4(4-3)+1(4-6)+5(1-2)\Big] -1 (0)\right| = \left|20(4-2-5)\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = 60</mrow>
        </md>.
        Above, we did not need to do any work to compute <m>C_{42}</m> because its
        <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix had two identical rows and thus had determinant <m>0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find all values of <m>h</m> so that the volume of the parallelepiped <m>P</m> formed by the
        four vectors below is equal to <m>15</m>.
        <md><mrow>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 0 \\ h \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
          v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
          v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\ 9\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
          v_4 = \begin{pmatrix}6 \\ 4 \\ 5 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        There are two values of <m>h</m> that work: <m>h = 5/82</m> and <m>h = 35/82</m>.  The volume
          of <m>P</m> is the absolute value of the determinant of the matrix with columns
          <m>v_1</m> through <m>v_4</m>, so we set it equal to <m>15</m> and solve for <m>h</m>.  We use the
          cofactor expansion along the first column.
        <md>
          <mrow>15 \amp  = \left|\det\begin{pmatrix}-2 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp 6</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 4</mrow>
          <mrow>h \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 5</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 9 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}\right|
                 = \left|-2C_{11} + 0 C_{21} + h C_{31} + 0 C_{41}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \left|-2(-1)^{1+1} \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 4</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 1 \amp 5</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 9 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} + h(-1)^{3+1}
                 \det\begin{pmatrix}4 \amp 0 \amp 6</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 2 \amp 4</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 9 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \left| -2 \Big[1(1-9\cdot 5)-2(1-0)+4(9-0)\Big]
                 + h \Big[4(2\cdot 1-9 \cdot 4)-0+6(1 \cdot 9 -0)\Big] \right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \left| -2(-44-2+36) + h(-136+54)\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  = \left|-2(-10) - 82h\right| = \left|20-82h\right|</mrow>
        </md>.
        Therefore, <m>\left|20-82h\right| = 15</m>.
      </p>
      <p>Case 1: <m>20-82h = 15</m>, so <m>82h = 5</m> and <m>h = 5/82</m>.</p>
      <p>Case 2: <m>-(20-82h) = 15</m>, so <m>-20+82h = 15</m>, thus <m>82h = 35</m>
        and <m>h = 35/82</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>R</m> be the triangle with vertices <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(1, 4)</m>, and <m>(10, 20)</m>.  Find
      the area of the new triangle <m>T(R)</m>, where <m>T: {\bf R}^2 \to {\bf R}^2</m> is the
      transformation <m>T(x) = \begin{pmatrix}8 \amp -3 \\ 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The area of <m>R</m> is
        <md><mrow>\dfrac{1}{2} \left| \det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 10 \\ 4 \amp 20\end{pmatrix}\right| = \dfrac{1}{2}|-20| = 10</mrow></md>.
        Let <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}8 \amp -3 \\ 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix}</m>, noting <m>|\det(A)| = 19</m>.  From the area formula from
          chapter 4, we know
        <md><mrow>\textup{Area}(T(R)) = \left|\det(A)\right|\cdot \textup{Area}(R)
            = 19(10) = 190</mrow></md>.
        Another way to do this problem is to transform the vertices of the original triangle
          into the new triangle: we compute <m>T\begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m>,
        <md><mrow>T\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 4\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}8 \amp -3 \\ 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 4\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-4 \\ 9\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
            T\begin{pmatrix}10 \\ 20\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}8 \amp -3 \\ 1 \amp 2\end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix}10 \\ 20\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}20 \\ 50\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, the vertices of <m>T(R)</m> are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(-4,9)</m>, and <m>(20,50)</m>.
        <md><mrow>\textup{Area}(T(R)) = \dfrac{1}{2}
            \left|\det\begin{pmatrix}-4 \amp 20 \\ 9 \amp 50\end{pmatrix}\right| = \dfrac{1}{2} |-200-180| = \dfrac{1}{2}(380)
            = 190</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>T: {\bf R}^2 \to {\bf R}^2</m> be the transformation
        <md><mrow>T(x) = \begin{pmatrix}7 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix} x</mrow></md>.
        Find the area of <m>T(C)</m>, where <m>C</m> is the circle
        of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        With <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}7 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}</m>, we use the area formula from section 4.3:
        <md><mrow>\textup{Area}(T(C)) = \left| \det(A) \right| \textup{Area}(C)
          = 21 \cdot (\pi(2^2)) = 84 \pi</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>If the area of a fish (in a photo) is <m>7</m> square inches, and if we apply the
        shear transformation <m>T(x) = \begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 13 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> to the photo, what is the area
        of the fish in the new photo?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>To get the area of the new fish, we multiply the original area by the
        determinant of the standard matrix for <m>T</m>.  The new area is
        <m>\det\begin{pmatrix}1 \amp 13 \\ 0 \amp 1\end{pmatrix} \cdot 7 = 1 \cdot 7 = 7</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> are vectors in <m>{\bf R}^2</m> that naturally form a
      parallelogram with area <m>10</m> square units.  What is the area of the
      parallelogram naturally formed by <m>3u</m> and <m>-v</m>. Justify this answer
      in two ways: first by using geometry, then by using properties of
      determinants.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>Geometrically: stretching <m>u</m> by a factor of three will scale the
        area by a factor of <m>3</m>, whereas multiplying <m>v</m> by <m>-1</m> will only
        change the direction of facing of the parallelogram.  Therefore, the
        resulting area is <m>3(10)=30</m>.</p>
      <p>
        Determinants: the area of the parallelogram determined by <m>u</m> and
          <m>v</m> is
        <md><mrow>\textup{Area} = \left|\det \begin{pmatrix}- \amp u \amp - \\ - \amp v \amp -\end{pmatrix}\right|=10 \textup{
               units}^2</mrow></md>.
        Scaling a row of a matrix by <m>3</m> multiplies the
           determinant by <m>3</m>, and multiplying a row by <m>-1</m> changes the
           determinant by <m>-1</m> (this will be irrelevant since we take the
           absolute value).
        <md><mrow>\textup{Area} = \left|\det \begin{pmatrix}- \amp 3u \amp - \\ - \amp -v \amp -\end{pmatrix}\right| =
             |10(3)(-1)| = 30 \textup{ units}^2</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>\setcounter{problem}{0}
\newpage
